  <h1>Is BlaBlaCar available in the USA? Here's what to use instead (2026)</h1>
  <div class="meta">Updated August 2026</div>

  <p class="lede"><strong>Short answer: no.</strong> BlaBlaCar (often typed <em>Bla Bla Car</em>) — Europe's massive city-to-city carpool platform — has never operated in the United States. Download the app here and you'll find zero American rides. But the thing BlaBlaCar does so well <em>does</em> exist in the US. Here's the landscape.</p>

  <h2>Why doesn't BlaBlaCar work in the US?</h2>
  <p>BlaBlaCar operates across Europe, Latin America, and parts of Asia, matching passengers with drivers already making long-distance trips who split their costs. It's never launched in the US — American intercity travel has historically defaulted to flights, buses, or driving alone, and no European operator has made the jump. That's left a real gap: millions of empty car seats travel between US cities every day.</p>

  <h2>US alternatives compared</h2>
  <div class="scroll">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>How it works</th><th>Safety / payment</th><th>Coverage</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td class="win">Seat Sherpa</td><td>App marketplace: book a seat with a verified driver already making the trip; split their gas (no profit, no surge)</td><td>ID + license-verified drivers, payment held securely until a pickup code confirms the trip, two-way ratings</td><td>California &amp; Nevada (SF, LA, San Diego, Las Vegas, Sacramento + more)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Craigslist rideshare</td><td>Classifieds board; arrange everything yourself</td><td>None — no verification, cash between strangers</td><td>Nationwide</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Facebook carpool groups</td><td>City/college groups; post and hope</td><td>Profile-only vetting, no payment protection</td><td>Patchy, group-by-group</td></tr>
      <tr><td>FlixBus / Greyhound</td><td>Scheduled buses</td><td>Commercial carrier</td><td>Nationwide, fixed routes/times</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Uber / Lyft</td><td>On-demand, priced per mile — impractical for intercity ($200–$400+ for SF–LA)</td><td>Commercial TNC</td><td>Metro areas</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <h3>🚗 The closest thing to BlaBlaCar in America</h3>
    <p>Seat Sherpa works on the same principle BlaBlaCar proved in Europe: drivers already making a trip share their empty seats, passengers pay a fair share of the real cost (typically $15–$55 a seat depending on distance), and nobody pays a commercial markup. Verified drivers, payment held safely until your trip starts, no surge — currently live across California and Nevada.</p>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">It's also on iOS and Android — and, uniquely, <strong>inside Apple Maps</strong>: search any California route, open the ride tab, and Seat Sherpa carpools appear alongside Uber and Lyft, usually at a fraction of the price.</p>
    <a class="btn" href="/routes">See routes &amp; prices</a>
  </div>

  <h2>What a seat actually costs</h2>
  <p>Because drivers can only recover their costs (capped at the IRS mileage rate split across seats), prices look like BlaBlaCar's, not Uber's: <a href="/routes/san-francisco-to-los-angeles">San Francisco → Los Angeles</a> runs $40–$55 a seat, <a href="/routes/los-angeles-to-las-vegas">LA → Vegas</a> $35–$50, <a href="/routes/los-angeles-to-san-diego">LA → San Diego</a> $15–$28. Compare that to $60–$150 flying or $200+ in an Uber.</p>

  <h2>Will BlaBlaCar ever launch in the US?</h2>
  <p>There's no announced plan. BlaBlaCar has spent the last few years deepening in Europe and Latin America — markets with dense city pairs, expensive fuel, and a strong intercity bus and rail culture to compete against. The US is a harder sell for them: longer distances, cheaper domestic flights, and a population that already owns cars. If you're waiting for the app to appear in the US App Store, it's not a good plan.</p>
  <p>The demand it would serve is real, though. Roughly three-quarters of American commuters drive alone, and intercity routes like San Francisco–Los Angeles carry thousands of half-empty cars every single day. That's the same inefficiency Bla Bla Car built a business on in Europe — the gap in the US isn't demand, it's that nobody filled it until recently.</p>

  <h2>BlaBlaCar vs Seat Sherpa, side by side</h2>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>BlaBlaCar (Europe)</th><th>Seat Sherpa (US)</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Model</td><td>Drivers post trips they're already taking; passengers book a seat</td><td class="win">Identical — cost-sharing, not a taxi service</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Pricing</td><td>Capped so drivers can't profit</td><td class="win">Capped at the IRS mileage rate, split across seats</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Verification</td><td>Profile + phone, ratings both ways</td><td class="win">Phone + government ID + driver's licence, ratings both ways</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Payment</td><td>In-app, held until the trip</td><td class="win">In-app, released only after a 4-digit pickup code confirms you're in the car</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Coverage</td><td>20+ countries, none in North America</td><td>California &amp; Nevada today</td></tr>
  </table></div>
  <p>The differences are mostly a matter of scale and market maturity. If you've used Bla Bla Car for a Paris–Lyon or Madrid–Barcelona run, the Seat Sherpa flow will feel immediately familiar: pick a trip, see the driver's rating and car, book a seat, meet at an agreed spot.</p>

  <h2>Visiting the US and used to BlaBlaCar?</h2>
  <p>A lot of people searching for Bla Bla Car in America are Europeans on a trip, or Americans who discovered it while living abroad. If that's you: the closest experience is a carpool marketplace on the routes you're likely to travel — San Francisco to Los Angeles, LA to Las Vegas, LA to San Diego. Book a seat the same way you would at home. Outside California and Nevada, your realistic options are still Amtrak, FlixBus, or Greyhound, none of which is bad — just slower and, on the popular routes, usually pricier than sharing a driver's fuel cost.</p>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <div class="faq"><h3>Does BlaBlaCar work in the United States?</h3><p>No — BlaBlaCar has never launched in the US. The app won't show any American rides.</p></div>
  <div class="faq"><h3>Is there Bla Bla Car in the USA?</h3><p>No. Bla Bla Car (however you spell it) operates in Europe, Latin America and parts of Asia, but not in the United States. The closest American equivalent is a city-to-city carpool marketplace like Seat Sherpa, currently covering California and Nevada.</p></div>
  <div class="faq"><h3>Will BlaBlaCar come to the US?</h3><p>There's no announced launch. The company has focused on Europe and Latin America, and has never signalled a North American rollout.</p></div>
  <div class="faq"><h3>What is the US equivalent of BlaBlaCar?</h3><p>A city-to-city carpool marketplace like Seat Sherpa: verified drivers already making the trip, cost-sharing seat prices, secure in-app payment. Currently across California &amp; Nevada.</p></div>
  <div class="faq"><h3>Is there a carpool app for long-distance road trips in the US?</h3><p>Yes — Seat Sherpa covers routes like SF–LA, LA–Vegas, and LA–San Diego. Craigslist and Facebook groups exist too, but without verification or payment protection.</p></div>

